# The verify gate's performance list for the gem5 host (C.GEM5_PERF_LIST):
# eight perf-size workloads, 34 to 65 M committed instructions each on
# gem5's O3 CPU (approx_insts in hosts/gem5/workloads.json holds the qemu
# count, within 1 percent of gem5's).
#
# G5 asks one question: does the mechanism move the metric the way the paper
# says? It is a direction check, not a score, as on the cbp2025 host.
#
# Why eight. The gate runs the list twice per attempt, feature on first and
# then feature off, one after the other (_run_performance in
# loop/plan_runner.py). So an attempt costs two waves, not one. Eight runs
# fit in one wave on the gem5 node's 30 slots (GEM5_HOST_RESOURCE), and a
# wave lasts as long as its slowest run. Measured on the clean tree
# (2026-09-23), these eight took 157 to 396 s each inside the 28-run
# cluster smoke, and 152 to 387 s each in the 28-run baseline recording.
# They took 221 to 521 s each in an 8-run baseline recording made while a
# gem5 build shared the node. gapbs_tc was the slowest every time. So the
# two waves cost about 13 minutes per attempt on a quiet node and about 18
# on a busy one, before the build. Each run gets at least
# GEM5_RUN_TIMEOUT_S (3600 s), whatever the entry's timeout_seconds says.
#
# Keep the feature-off wave: leave the entry's baseline source at its
# default, measure_feature_off. Source "recorded" compares against the
# recorded baseline's top-level means, and those cover the list that made
# the recording. Today that is all 28 workloads (hosts/gem5/baselines/
# iso-64KiB.json, recorded over gem5-all.list), not these eight, so the
# comparison would mix two different sets of workloads.
#
# Why these eight. They cover all five programs, and they lean toward code
# whose branches depend on data values, because sR predicts from register
# values.
#   gapbs_bfs    frontier checks on an irregular graph
#   gapbs_sssp   bucket and distance compares in delta stepping
#   gapbs_tc     sorted-list intersection, the classic hard-to-predict loop
#   bzip2        block sorting and Huffman coding on generated text
#   zstd         binary-tree match finding (btlazy2) on generated text
#   lua_fannkuch the most branch-bound Lua script, run by an interpreter
#   lua_word_freq string scanning, string-keyed tables and a sort
#   sqlite       B-tree inserts, joins, grouping, sorting and updates
#
# Left out, and still in gem5-all.list: gapbs_cc, gapbs_pr and gapbs_bc,
# which repeat the graph kind of work above. lua_binary_trees, which mostly
# measures the allocator and the garbage collector. lua_nbody and
# lua_spectral_norm, the regular end of the set, where the branches are
# mostly loop control.
gapbs_bfs
gapbs_sssp
gapbs_tc
bzip2
zstd
lua_fannkuch
lua_word_freq
sqlite
